WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.510 align:start position:0%
 
so they are trying to get back to to  where they were hopefully that's the  plan it's time to dissect all of the  most difficult umbrella academy scenes  to film including but not limited to  cornfield explosions amputations and  seven hours of blood curdling screams  definitely take some discipline take  some hard work number one the stars of  this show tend to put their vocal cords  through the ringer when filming intense  scenes in this example we're talking  about aiden gallagher who plays five  having to chop his literal arm off  five's arm needs to get cut off but he  needs to survive it so we had to figure  out a way of like him applying a  tourniquet with one hand aiden lost his  voice multiple times when filming this  scene and then again in post-production  while doing recording replacement  dialogue i blew my voice out quite a  couple of times shooting that and also  an adr number two  you'd be surprised to learn that one of  the most difficult things to shoot in  season three was running on a treadmill  tom hopper who plays luther had a less  than great time getting into character  on those things we look like we're old  men on like we've just broken our legs  and we're just getting back into rehab  and to make matters worse and actually  when we had to do these tighter shots we  had to pretend to run number three  this was by far one of the cast's least  favorite scenes to film it makes sense  why it would be shot at the very end  that was a very painful day we all had  to stare up into these bright lights and  scream for pretty much seven hours  straight some of the actors who didn't  have to do the scene expressed just how  grateful they were to be left out  i have to say i felt very fortunate that  i was dead yeah some of them also  expressed concern for their fellow  co-stars  i was worried for a few of you a couple  times  because like everyone would start to go  like blue in the face number four  sometimes the outfit you've gotta wear  to work can make or break your entire  day especially if the entire thing is  made out of leather and doesn't bend at  the joints the cast of umbrella academy  had a super difficult time performing in  these tight leather jumpsuits oh it was  so tight no screaming  just leather not comfy didn't uh bend in  the knees or in the joints number five  okay obviously we loved watching this  dance-off play out but some of the less  coordinated cast had quite a difficult  time getting it down so who's the best  dancer on the crew  definitely not this guy  according to elliot the dance came a bit  more naturally for emmy raver lampman  and david castaneda but for him and the  rest of the crew rehearsal days were  much longer jake epstein shares how they  even studied kevin bacon's moves for a  little assistance they're like get get  kevin bacon get the screen  so we'd like watch him and then try to  imitate it number six who could forget  this iconic season two moment in the  third episode of the season we get to  see a great example of anya's powers as  an entire cornfield is blown to bits fun  fact all of that corn was grown  specifically for that scene the thing  that made it so difficult to shoot was  just how unpredictable growing crops can  be in an interview with slash films the  director steven sergic talks about how  stressed he was when visiting the  cornfields three weeks before shooting  and the corn had just barely started  growing i was sick he said i didn't  think it was going to be ready but  luckily farming specialists stepped in  and planted genetically manipulated  seeds three weeks later the corn was  much larger than they ever expected and  they had to re-light the entire scene  surgic told comic beets i was thinking  the corn is going to get up to three and  a half four feet like very tall grass  no we go there and it was eight and nine  feet it was insane it was so high that  it was dark inside when you walked  through the rows  number seven interestingly enough aidan  gallagher's most difficult scene was the  first scene he ever shot for the pilot  no not because of a wardrobe malfunction  or losing his voice a dozen times but  because he was so nervous when i jumped  forward and got stuck in the future  do you know what i found no  nothing in an interview with looper  aiden discussed the scene where five  talks to vanya about the end of the  world as an actor the first day of any  production is the most stressful it's  the test to figure out and see if the  chemistry works between the characters  and you can feel out what direction  everybody is going in well in our  personal opinion we'd say the chemistry  is very apparent great job aiden number  eight we've got a lot going on in this  season 2 episode when 5 arrives in the  1960s he sees his siblings fighting  soviet soldiers right before a massive  nuclear blast hits the streets this  scene was actually supposed to be shot  in one take but due to how dangerous it  ended up being they couldn't even film  it on the street there were a ton of  logistical issues while attempting to  bring this sequence to life in an  interview with before and afters everett  burrell the visual effects supervisor  explains we couldn't control the street  for four or five days or two weeks solid  to build and prep so instead we built a  360 degree blue screen on our back lot  in toronto we called it the blue screen  playground he also shared the sequence  was one of the hardest most complicated  things i've ever done  number nine the action-packed and highly  entertaining fight scene between hazel  and cha-cha might look effortless but it  actually took months of training i think  they'll be surprised  uh to see us  doing hand-to-hand combat  and shooting guns cameron britton who  plays hazel shared in an interview with  inverse some fun facts about working  with mary j blige with mary we trained  for a couple of days before we went in  and shot it and she had been training at  six in the morning almost every day for  months she came ready to fight cameron  really took a liking to fight scenes  especially one in particular that he  says was the most difficult one to date  number 10 diego is definitely a force to  be reckoned with even for a seasoned  fighter like hazel in his interview with  inverse cameron gives us a bit of  insight into what it was like to film  that scene with david castaneda david is  so fast that my reactions are just  trying to keep up with him he explained  how he was essentially just there for  david to unload a barrage of punches  why are you here  well  yeah go stop number eleven at the end of  season two  there is a major face-off with the  commission's agent on a farm but  something you may not know is that there  was a major setback due to the weather  conditions in his interview with before  and afters everett burrell shares some  insight on this snowy debacle half the  scene was shot out on normal grass out  in the field and then it snowed and it  never stopped snowing so for half the  scene we had to roto-half the actors out  and put in grass and then the other half  of the scene we had to do the opposite  we had to put snow in for the reverse  shots would you have ever guessed the  snow wasn't part of the plan number 12.  a fun fact we learned is that elliott  paige learned to play the violin  specifically for the show in an  interview with pop buzz elliott shared  that he can play a slightly decent  twinkle twinkle little star but that's  about it he even had to have a teacher  on set who he says was very patient with  him apparently though even with  elliott's twinkle twinkle little star  knowledge he still had to have a  stand-in for the more complicated violin  scenes his stand-in was a 16 year old  prodigy named imogen sloss  the umbrella academy will never stop  impressing us with complicated intense  and beautiful sequences it's clear how  much heart and hard work goes into  creating each season what were you most  surprised to find out about the show  you

